i have edited the objects using kubectl edit, set, scale etc
now i want to make it permanet, how?

To make your live cluster changes permanent, you need to export the live configuration back into a YAML file and apply it declaratively using kubectl apply. This brings your local files and version control back into sync with the cluster.


Step 1: Export the Live Configuration

Run kubectl get with the -o yaml flag to save the current state of the resource to your local machine.
• Command: kubectl get deployment nginx -o yaml > nginx-deployment.yaml

• Note: You can do this for any resource you modified (service, replicaset, configmap, etc.).

Step 2: Clean Up Cluster-Generated Metadata

When you export a live resource, Kubernetes includes temporary runtime fields that should not be stored in your permanent configuration file. Open nginx-deployment.yaml and delete these sections:

• metadata.uid, metadata.resourceVersion, metadata.generation, and metadata.creationTimestamp
• metadata.managedFields (This is often a long block tracking field ownership; delete the entire block).
• status (The entire status: section at the bottom of the file, which tracks live pod counts and conditions).
(Tip: If you have the kubectl-neat plugin installed, you can run kubectl get deployment nginx -o yaml | kubectl neat > nginx-deployment.yaml to strip all of these fields automatically.)

Step 3: Commit and Apply Declaratively

Once your file is clean, establish it as your permanent source of truth.

• Save to Version Control: Commit the updated nginx-deployment.yaml file to your Git repository.
• Sync with kubectl apply: Run kubectl apply -f nginx-deployment.yaml.
Running kubectl apply updates the last-applied-configuration annotation on the live object, ensuring future updates won't trigger conflict warnings or wipe out your changes.

