 if you do not have local YAML files for them yet, and they lack the tracking annotation required for declarative management.
To make them permanent, you must extract their live configurations into new YAML files, strip out the cluster-generated runtime data, and adopt them into kubectl apply.

Step 1: Export Each Live Object to a File

Save the current state of every resource you created and modified into local YAML files.

• Export Deployment: kubectl get deployment <name> -o yaml > deployment.yaml
• Export Service: kubectl get service <name> -o yaml > service.yaml
• Export Everything at Once (Optional): kubectl get deploy,svc -o yaml > all-resources.yaml

Step 2: Strip Out Cluster-Specific Metadata

Open each exported YAML file in your text editor and delete the temporary fields that Kubernetes generated when the object was running. Keep only apiVersion, kind, metadata (with just name, namespace, and labels), and spec.
Delete these specific fields:
• metadata.uid
• metadata.resourceVersion
• metadata.generation
• metadata.creationTimestamp
• metadata.managedFields (Delete this entire block).
• status (Delete the entire status: block at the very bottom of the file).

Step 3: Adopt the Files with kubectl apply

Now that you have clean configuration files, tell Kubernetes to start tracking them declaratively.

• Run Apply: kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml (and repeat for your other files).

• What This Does: Even though the objects already exist, 
running kubectl apply attaches the kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration annotation to them in the cluster.
(Note: You might see a one-time warning saying kubectl apply should be used on resource created by either kubectl create --save-config or kubectl apply. This is completely normal and expected during this transition—it simply confirms that Kubernetes is adding the tracking annotation for the first time.)

Step 4: Save to Version Control

Commit these clean YAML files to a Git repository. From this point forward, make all future changes by editing these files and running kubectl apply -f <filename>.yaml instead of using imperative commands.

