if you created the objects using kubectl create -f config.yaml, your local config.yaml file is now out of date, and the live objects in your cluster do not have the last-applied-configuration tracking annotation that kubectl apply expects.

To make your live changes permanent, you need to overwrite your outdated config.yaml with the current live state, clean up the cluster-generated runtime fields, and transition the file to kubectl apply.


Step 1: Overwrite config.yaml with the Live State

Export the live configuration from the cluster directly into your existing config.yaml file (or a temporary file first so you can compare them).
• Single resource in file: kubectl get <kind> <name> -o yaml > config.yaml
• Multiple resources in file: kubectl get deploy,svc -o yaml > config.yaml (Adjust the resource types to match what is inside your original file).


