Step 3: Transition to kubectl apply
Because you originally used kubectl create -f, Kubernetes is not tracking your local file changes yet. Run kubectl apply once to adopt the updated config.yaml as your permanent, declarative source of truth.
• Run Apply: kubectl apply -f config.yaml
• Expect a One-Time Warning: Kubernetes will display a warning saying Warning: resource ... is missing the kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration annotation. This is normal—it means Kubernetes is attaching the tracking annotation now so future updates work cleanly.

Step 4: Commit to Version Control

Save and commit your updated config.yaml to Git. From now on, avoid using kubectl create, edit, set, or scale on these objects—instead, edit config.yaml directly and run kubectl apply -f config.yaml.